  <title>Finance charges</title>

  <p><guiseq><gui>Customer</gui><gui>Finance charge</gui></guiseq> charges
  interest on invoices a customer has not paid on time. Only customers who owe
  something are listed.</p>

  <steps>
    <item><p>Choose the <gui>Customer</gui>. <gui>Balance</gui> is what they
    owe.</p></item>
    <item><p>The list shows each invoice that is past the customer's <link
    xref="customer_terms">terms</link>, with what is still unpaid and the
    <gui>Interest</gui> on it. For a cash-only customer every unpaid invoice is
    overdue. <gui>Finance Charge</gui> is the total interest.</p></item>
    <item><p>Click <gui>View statement</gui> to see the finance charge
    statement before posting it.</p></item>
    <item><p>Click <gui>Post finance charge</gui> and print it.</p></item>
  </steps>

  <p>The interest on an invoice is a month's interest, at the finance rate in
  <link xref="settings"/>, for each whole month the invoice is past its terms,
  and at least one month. Posting adds the finance charge to the customer's
  account as a charge of its own, which is listed with their unpaid invoices
  and paid like one. Finance charges are not charged interest
  themselves.</p>

  <note><p>Unlike an invoice, a finance charge is only posted when it is
  printed. Clicking <gui>Cancel</gui> in the print dialog posts
  nothing.</p></note>

  <p><gui>Add payment</gui> opens <link xref="customer_payment"/> for the
  chosen customer, to record a payment before charging interest.</p>

</page>
